// SOS CHILD: REACHING FOR A RIGHT ITS MASK LEFT OUT (sawos design 4, as ruled
// Aug 29).
//
// `root-server-oversteps` is this program one privilege level up: a process that
// tries to do something it was not given, so that the refusal can be READ rather
// than assumed. The difference is what stops it. Root oversteps into the
// KERNEL's memory and the hardware refuses; this child oversteps into an
// AUTHORITY and the rights word refuses, which is the same discipline enforced
// by the other of the two mechanisms SOS has.
//
//   SOS childover: I can print, so Debug travelled
//   SOS childover: now trying to stop the machine
//   SOS: process fault: access denied process=1     <- the CHILD, not root
//   SOS: process teardown ... process=1
//
// **THE FIRST LINE IS HALF THE CLAIM.** A keep mask is not a wall around a
// process, it is a SET: `Debug` was named, so this program has a voice, and the
// very fact that it can announce what it is about to try is the positive half of
// the attenuation. A case that only showed the refusal could not tell "the mask
// worked" from "the handle was broken".
//
// **AND THE FAULT NAMES PROCESS 1.** Overstepping ends the process that
// overstepped — design 178's faults ruling, applied to a rights bit that was
// MASKED OFF rather than never minted — and the machine keeps running, which is
// what lets the launcher print its own line afterwards. A kernel that stopped on
// any process's death would end the run here.
//
// It names no architecture and would build for either profile unchanged.

import sos.{System, SosStatus}

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    // The same adoption root writes. What differs is only the rights word behind
    // the handle — this one is a sibling a launcher minted through a mask.
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS childover: I can print, so Debug travelled\n")
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS childover: now trying to stop the machine\n")

    // REFUSED, and it does not return. `Shutdown` was absent BY SILENCE from the
    // keep mask, and a masked bit is not recoverable by any sequence of ops: a
    // mint's result is a subset of its source.
    let _ = system.shutdown(0)

    // Unreachable. A child that somehow got past the line above has nowhere to
    // go, so it spins inside its own granted region and the harness catches it
    // as a timeout rather than as a pass.
    while { }
}
